One finding came from validating the tools rather than from the data: plugging the in-sample skewness into the Probabilistic Sharpe Ratio and applying Bonferroni produced false discoveries in {{e1.mc.gbm.rates.any_bonferroni_psr.rate:.0%}} of Gaussian null worlds (5% allowed). The per-variant tests therefore use the classical t-test instead. The choice matters for the headline and is stated here for that reason: with the PSR-based p-values, {{e3.net.survivors_psr.holm:,}} of the {{e3.n_variants:,}} variants would pass Holm and {{e3.net.survivors_psr.bh:,}} would pass Benjamini-Hochberg. The cause and the fix are described in [REPORT.md](REPORT.md) sections 5.1 and 5.3 and in [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md).

